---
/**
 * Shown right after SKYE is installed on a new site (/switcher). SKYE
 * can't set SharePoint ACLs via Graph, so this explains that Members can
 * currently edit SKYE's files and links to the skye_data folder's
 * permission settings. fillPermissionsStep() (src/lib/routing/siteSwitcher.ts)
 * fills the site/library names, sets the link href (or removes the link
 * when none resolved), and wires the "finished" button.
 */
---

<section class="skye-site-switcher skye-permissions-step" data-state id="step-permissions" hidden>
  <h2>Manage permissions</h2>
  <p>
    SKYE is set up on <span data-slot="site-name"></span>, in a "skye_data"
    folder inside the <span data-slot="library-name"></span> library. Right
    now both Owners and Members of <span data-slot="site-name"></span> can
    edit SKYE's forms, views, and config there. Most setups restrict
    Members to view-only — an owner does that by breaking permission
    inheritance on the skye_data folder in SharePoint (the link below opens
    that folder's permission settings directly).
  </p>
  <a
    class="skye-permissions-step__link"
    data-el="manage-link"
    target="_blank"
    rel="noopener noreferrer"
    hidden>Open the skye_data folder's permission settings ↗</a>
  <button class="skye-permissions-step__done" data-el="done" type="button">
    I'm finished setting permissions
  </button>
</section>
